Kirby and the rainbow curse is a strange  game and not in the best way its  mechanics are similar to 2005 Nintendo  DS title Kirby canvas cars  it takes its predecessors unique  mechanics but pairs them down into a  game that alternates between boring and  frustrating rainbow curse starts with an  evil force draining all color and life  out of Kirby's homeland so he sets out  through an alternate dimension to stomp  enemies collect stars and get the color  back players guide Kirby through seven  worlds each with three separate stages  and one boss battle on their way to stop  the evil but this isn't the kind of  Kirby game you'd expect  there's no sucking up enemies no  stomping on their heads under the  traditional combat movement instead you  move Kirby using the gamepad stylus  trying rainbow lines on the screen for  him to roll along the direction you draw  the line in is the direction Kirby will  take tapping on Kirby will make him move  faster in the direction he's facing  collecting 100 stars and then holding  down the stylus will make her be  enlarged himself and move faster  allowing him to break thicker materials  like metal and damage larger enemies  it's too simple and too unwieldy Kirby's  physics are floating in rainbow curse  making for a rather uncomfortable  experience corralling him stopping him  from moving getting him to quickly  change directions is a pain you have to  draw your lines quickly and in the right  direction or you could roll Kirby into  certain doom and if Kirby is already on  the move snagging him is even more of a  pain if you run out of magic paint to  draw those lines you're in trouble  death comes easy and rainbow curse if  you have difficulty adapting to this way  of movement  there are also a handful of dedicated  levels in which Kirby becomes a torpedo  shooting submarine or a plane or a  slow-moving tank these levels are the  most infuriating as again you can only  use stylist taps and swipes to guide him  for the submarine tapping in an area  both shoots missiles straight ahead and  moves Kirby in that direction in order  to guide your missiles you need to draw  Rainbow lines they can fly along same  goes for plain Kirby he needs rainbow  tracks to guide him this is where the  difficulty spike and rainbow curse is  most apparent it expects you to adapt to  drawing lines and being a vehicle early  on but doesn't give you much room to  master throwing you into harder  situations to quickly beyond sliding  along rainbows and rolling into enemies  normal Kirby can't do anything no  inhaling no turning to a brick no spin  attack nothing these limitations make  gameplay boring most of the time and  frustrating in the more difficult boss  battles you never really feel like you  have full control of Kirby  it's maddening levels do get  progressively harder as the game goes on  and some of the latter ones are better  with friends rainbow curse lets you play  with up to three companions as Waddle  Dean Kirby's pal waddle dee is more  useful than Kirby in many situations  waddle dee can jump hover for small  periods of time and slash enemies having  a waddle dee or two runs support while  Kirby floats around the screen  collecting stars provides a safety net  playing some of the latter stages solo  feels almost a little scary  luckily the game is generous with 1ups  and health regeneration items but that  doesn't make failure hurt any less  losing a life when you're trying to get  Kirby to safety or avoid an enemy feels  unfair when your movement is bound to a  single track going in one direction it's  difficult to get some hidden  collectibles but honestly there's no  incentive to get them at all nor is  there any fun and playing in challenge  mode where you are given a set of four  stages that lasts 15 seconds each in  each stage you must maneuver through  obstacles to reach a treasure chest fail  to reach the chest in more than one room  and you lose the challenge these rooms  are bite-sized pieces of the tamer  stretches of some levels and their  rewards in-game collectibles aren't  enough to justify the time spent unless  you're a completionist rainbow curse  does have amiibo functionality but it  barely makes a difference with gameplay  tapping a Kirby amiibo to the gamepad  allows you to use kirby star dash at any  time using King Dedede gives Kirby 2  extra health bars and the Meta Knight  amiibo gives Kirby a power boost  but if Kirby dies once loses just 1 life  the bonus is lost and you can only use  one amiibo once per day with rainbow  curse the functionality feels forced in  honestly and is ineffective in the long  run Kirby and the rainbow curse is an OK  game it's beautiful look at filled with  gorgeous bright claymation style  environments and swathes of color but  the beauty is skin-deep its mechanic is  monotonous and at time unreliable making  for a game based on trial and error  that's not very interesting it doesn't  matter how seasoned to vet you are here  Kirby's difficult handling and strict  movement make things unfun
 

